WEBVTT
TITLE Transactions and Concurrency Control — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Two transactions, one account balance. Run them one after the other and the arithmetic is right.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Interleave them and the second read happens before the first write lands. One update is lost.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The fix is not "run serially". It is "run concurrently, but only in orders a serial run would allow".

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
That demand has a name — serialisability — and it is the centre of this lesson.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Atomicity: all of the transaction or none of it. Enforced by the log and undo on restart.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Consistency: a transaction takes one valid state to another. Enforced by constraints and by the application.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Isolation: concurrent execution must look like some serial order. Enforced by the concurrency-control protocol.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Durability: a committed write survives a crash. Enforced by force-writing the log before commit is acknowledged.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Two operations conflict when they are on the same item, from different transactions, and at least one writes.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Three shapes: read-write, write-read, write-write. Read-read is the one that is not a conflict.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A conflict pins an order: the earlier operation must stay earlier in any equivalent serial schedule.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So a schedule is conflict serialisable exactly when those pinned orders are mutually consistent.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
GATE CSE 2014 Set 3 gives three transactions and two schedules. One vertex per transaction.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Schedule S1 gives T2 to T3 on Y, T3 to T1 on X, and T2 to T1 on Z. No cycle.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Schedule S2 reverses one pair on Z, and now T1 to T2 and T2 to T1 both appear. Cycle.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So only S1 is conflict serialisable — option A. The graph, not intuition, decided it.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Conflict serialisability is sufficient, not necessary. Some schedules are equivalent to a serial one and still have a cycle.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Take T1 reading and writing A, and T2 and T3 each writing A without reading it.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
In r1(A), w2(A), w1(A), w3(A) the graph has a cycle, yet T1 reads the initial value and T3 writes last — exactly as in the serial order T1, T2, T3.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The containment is strict: conflict serialisable sits inside view serialisable, which sits inside serialisable.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
GATE CSE 2017 Set 2 asks how many conflict-serialisable schedules two four-operation transactions can form.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Total interleavings: choose 4 of the 8 positions for T1. That is 70.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Conflicts can only happen on Y, because X belongs only to T1 and Z only to T2.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Enumeration gives 54. One schedule has T1 wholly before T2; the other 53 are equivalent to T2 then T1.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Recoverable: if T2 read a value T1 wrote, T1 must commit before T2 does.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Cascadeless: you may only read values whose writer has already committed.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Strict: nobody may read or write an item until its last writer has committed.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The ladder is one-way: strict implies cascadeless implies recoverable, and neither converse holds.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Two-phase locking: one growing phase where locks are only taken, then one shrinking phase where they are only released.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A transaction locks an item at or before its first access and releases it at or after its last.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
So two transactions may not need the same item over overlapping spans. S1 fails this, though it is conflict serialisable.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
2PL gives conflict serialisability but not deadlock freedom; strict 2PL also gives strictness and recovery.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Timestamp ordering gives every transaction a number and keeps two per item: the largest read time and the largest write time.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
A read is refused if a later transaction has already written the item. A write is refused if a later transaction has already read it.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The Thomas write rule: if a later write already landed, silently skip this one instead of aborting.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Timestamp ordering never deadlocks, because nobody waits. It aborts and restarts instead.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: read what is asked. Conflict, view, recoverability and locking are four different tests.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: list the operations in order, and write each one as transaction, action, item.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: add an edge for every conflicting pair, then look only for a cycle.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Steps four and five: for counting, count interleavings then filter; for locking, check spans for overlap.

